WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
My AI agent forgot a critical

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.100
constraint I gave it 200 turns

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.340
ago because it didn't fit in

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:08.380
its context window.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.860
To solve this I built trace

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.020
a database which organizes itself proven

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.900
at 100 million token context and

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.160
actively scaling beyond

00:00:21.224 --> 00:00:23.764
It self organizes into a hierarchy

00:00:23.764 --> 00:00:26.964
and when retrieving data, it activates

00:00:26.964 --> 00:00:28.604
less than 1% of the

00:00:28.604 --> 00:00:31.944
corpus for fast and accurate retrieval.

00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:35.884
Because of this, my AI agents

00:00:35.884 --> 00:00:37.884
never solve the same problem twice.

00:00:38.664 --> 00:00:40.724
They look into their memory and

00:00:40.724 --> 00:00:44.064
find similar problems and use the

00:00:44.064 --> 00:00:45.264
same techniques here.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.210
To test this on real-world tasks

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.410
I built Anima. It is a

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:52.230
harness which plugs into your existing

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:54.870
coding agents like Muse code or

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.270
Claude code via the CLI and

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.890
it uses trace to provide the

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.650
right context at the right place

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:01.430
and time.

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:05.450
It never forgets anything. Anima uses

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:07.850
a hard hierarchy of agents like

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.310
architect agents and builder agents to

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:13.630
get work done more efficiently and

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.850
with higher quality.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.530
It allows the architect to operate

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.830
mostly on the architectural plane, defining

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:24.630
the system or fixing bugs instead

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:25.790
of doing the grunt work.

00:01:26.332 --> 00:01:28.552
It has separate agents for persistent

00:01:28.552 --> 00:01:31.832
constraint enforcement and other things. It

00:01:31.832 --> 00:01:33.432
also comes with a visual dashboard

00:01:33.432 --> 00:01:36.272
which shows the state-space tree of

00:01:36.272 --> 00:01:37.672
all the projects you are working

00:01:37.672 --> 00:01:38.092
on.

00:01:39.292 --> 00:01:41.012
It tracks all the state in

00:01:41.012 --> 00:01:42.992
one place and allows us to

00:01:42.992 --> 00:01:44.952
revert from a weak branch and

00:01:44.952 --> 00:01:46.392
try a new branch while learning

00:01:46.392 --> 00:01:47.272
from our mistakes.

00:01:47.952 --> 00:01:50.149
This allows the human to stop

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:52.149
acting as the manager and act

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:54.609
as the lead architect pruning the

00:01:54.609 --> 00:01:57.629
state space tree and enforcing constraints.

00:01:59.089 --> 00:02:01.249
To provide hands to ANIMA, I

00:02:01.249 --> 00:02:04.609
built Reflex. It makes computer use

00:02:04.609 --> 00:02:07.283
more capable and efficient. It uses

00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:08.403
a ladder of escalation,

00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:11.603
using cheaper techniques like OS accessibility

00:02:11.603 --> 00:02:14.663
suites and OCR models fine-tuned

00:02:14.663 --> 00:02:17.343
on understanding UI elements like button

00:02:17.343 --> 00:02:18.543
on understanding a

00:02:18.543 --> 00:02:20.703
screen can scroll before calling a

00:02:20.703 --> 00:02:23.023
VLM and it uses its understanding

00:02:23.023 --> 00:02:25.143
to make the VLM calls cheaper

00:02:25.143 --> 00:02:27.023
when they are made. It doesn't

00:02:27.023 --> 00:02:28.643
feed the entire screen. It feeds

00:02:28.643 --> 00:02:30.223
parts of the screen to the

00:02:30.223 --> 00:02:30.463
VLM.

00:02:31.783 --> 00:02:33.503
It also uses a larger model

00:02:33.503 --> 00:02:36.023
like Muse Spark 1.3 as

00:02:36.023 --> 00:02:38.743
the planner for long context reasoning

00:02:38.743 --> 00:02:41.663
and healing from a failed trajectory.

00:02:42.683 --> 00:02:45.643
Live demos, benchmarks and technical papers

00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:46.863
can be found on my GitHub.
